nodes/node_projection_work_events/0001_create_work_events.sql	nodes/node_projection_work_events/0002_work_events_summary_bound.sql	OMN-16180	0001 shipped an internal LAN address in a header comment. omnimarket blocks that literal (leaked-literals gate OMN-10580) while its node-migration-vendor-parity-gate requires the omnimarket source file to be byte-identical to this vendored copy, so the address cannot be scrubbed on one side only. The scrub is COMMENT-ONLY -- every executable statement in 0001 is byte-unchanged -- and the recorded content_sha256 is updated with it. CORRECTED OMN-17139: the safety claim originally recorded here was false. It read '0001 has only ever been applied by hand (psql -f) ... never through run-forward-migrations.sh, so no onex_application_migration_manifest row records its prior checksum on any lane.' Both halves fail. The dev lane applied 0001 THROUGH run-forward-migrations.sh at 2026-08-30 04:59:57Z and recorded it in platform_catalog.schema_migrations (checksum cba8013e..., provenance file:nodes/node_projection_work_events/0001_create_work_events.sql) -- two hours before this rewrite merged. And onex_application_migration_manifest is not the relation that gates: it is a TEMP table bootstrap.sql builds from the checked-in TSV, so a to_regclass() probe against it reads clean on every lane and proves nothing. The gate is migration_is_applied() in run-forward-migrations.sh, reading platform_catalog.schema_migrations; it hard-failed the dev lane at dev tip. The rewrite itself is genuinely comment-only (every executable statement byte-unchanged, proven by scripts/migrations/prove_migration_revision_equivalence.py) and is admitted by the declaration in _ledger/verified-canonical-adoptions.tsv, not by this row. The successor named here additionally closes a real gap -- summary is bounded at 2000 chars in ModelWorkEventRow but the column was bare TEXT.
nodes/node_projection_delegation/0031_delegation_events_tenant_id_to_uuid.sql	nodes/node_projection_delegation/0032_delegation_events_tenant_id_uuid_via_registry.sql	OMN-16930	0031 resolved tenant identity from a CLOSED THREE-VALUE LITERAL CASE with no ELSE. The live table holds 169 rows across SIX writing slugs (census 2026-08-29), nine of them outside that map -- including t-replayfix, a real active externally-owned customer provisioned eight days AFTER 0031 was authored. The map is incomplete BY CONSTRUCTION: omninode_cloud.public.tenants grows on every beta signup (39 tenants and counting), and a migration is immutable once applied, so no literal map authored at any point in time can stay correct. 0031's bytes CANNOT be revised. The .201 dev lane's platform_catalog.schema_migrations holds it with checksum 79ee3b021d0a04088b2f733fa0558ea110b2a6f75b4fb338abe9c5c123f74442 kind content_sha256, byte-identical to both committed copies and to this ledger's own manifest row; bootstrap.sql raises 'conflicting migration checksum in canonical node history' on any divergence (the OMN-16705 class). Four-lane sweep 2026-08-29: dev PRESENT and column already uuid, stability-test absent and column still text, prod and judge carry no platform_catalog ledger at all. 0032 re-expresses the conversion additively and resolves identity by JOINing tenant_registry_mirror -- a runtime-populated relation fed by node_projection_tenant_registry from onex.tenant.events -- instead of inlining any literal. No slug appears in its transform expression. It is a no-op on the dev lane (column already uuid) and fail-closed everywhere else: an unresolvable slug aborts, naming the slugs and stating that the projection has not caught up. Operator ruling 2026-08-29, verbatim: 'Hold + fix mechanism'. BOTH 0031 and 0032 remain in the OMN-15349 fence baseline until the operator un-gates.
